<h2>The allegations</h2>
<p>The ED's case, recorded in ECIR/DNSZ0/04/2023, is that the two brothers ran a dark-web drug trafficking operation and laundered the sale proceeds through Bitcoin. Banmeet Singh had been arrested in the United Kingdom in 2019 on a United States extradition request, entered a plea in the US in January 2024, and was released in April 2024 — only to be arrested in India on arrival, in May 2024. The ED alleges a transfer of 4,250 BTC to his brother Parvinder, which remains unsecured; Parvinder surrendered 268.22 BTC, valued at about Rs 130.48 crore as of May 2024. Prosecution complaints were filed and cognizance taken in July 2024.</p>

<h2>How the remand dispute arose</h2>
<p>In May 2026 the Supreme Court set aside the cognizance order against Parvinder and directed fresh proceedings from that stage; the High Court did the same for Banmeet days later. Remand ran to 6 June 2026 — a holiday, followed by a Sunday. On 8 June the accused opposed remand through counsel, but only custody warrants were signed and no remand order was passed. On 12 June the Special Judge (PMLA), Dehradun, rejected their applications and remanded both to custody. Those orders — and the legality of the custody in the gap — were the heart of the challenge under Section 528 BNSS.</p>

<h2>The Court's answers</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Remand without cognizance is lawful.</strong> On the authority of Suresh Kumar Bhikamchand Jain, (2013) 3 SCC 77, SFIO v. Rahul Modi, (2023) 15 SCC 311, and Judgebir Singh v. NIA, (2023) 17 SCC 48, a remand order may precede cognizance on the complaint; when cognizance is taken in a complaint case was left as academic.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The 8 June lapse was not fatal.</strong> Jigar @ Jimmy Adatiya, (2023) 6 SCC 484, requires the accused's presence for custody extension — but remand was opposed through counsel that day, and no indefeasible right to release accrued.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>A valid later remand cures the gap.</strong> Following Naranjan Singh Nathawan, (1952) 1 SCC 118, and Pranab Chatterjee, (1970) 3 SCC 926, the reasoned remand of 12 June 2026 validated the custody; Article 22 non-communication cases such as Vihaan Kumar, (2025) 5 SCC 799, were distinguished on their facts.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Bail refused on the merits of the record.</strong> The transnational scale, the unsecured 4,250 BTC and the foreign remittances outweighed the custody-length argument; there is no fixed rule by which delay alone ripens into bail.</div>
</div>
<p>Both Section 528 BNSS petitions were dismissed and both bail applications rejected.</p>

<h2>What the judgment signals for PMLA defence work</h2>
<p>First, remand-stage technicalities are a narrowing field: unless the defect is of the Article 22(1) communication variety — where Vihaan Kumar bites — courts are likely to treat later reasoned remands as curative. Second, custody-length arguments in economic offences must be built on trial-delay attribution, not bare arithmetic; here the Court found the scale of unsecured crypto-assets decisive the other way. Third, crypto-tracing is now central to both sides of a PMLA bail hearing: what has been surrendered, what remains unsecured, and what that says about flight risk and further laundering.</p>
